---
title: "Cross-reference targets"
---

# Reading affordances

A long document is full of references that point elsewhere: "see @fig-flow", "as
@sec-why explains". Every block carries a stable id, so a reference always resolves
to a real target rather than a guessed anchor.

## Why it works {#sec-why}

A cross-reference target can be a heading anchored with `{#id}`, or a code cell
labelled with a cell-level `label:` — both are indexed the same way while the
document is parsed.

See @sec-why above, or the diagram in @fig-flow below.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-flow
%%| fig-cap: "A reference resolves to its target by id."
flowchart LR
  Reference -->|resolves| Target
```

A footnote[^aside] resolves the same way, by its own stable id.

[^aside]: This note lives at the document's end, reached the same way any other
reference is: by following its link.
